mpletion of the proof of the problem\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n*If \\(k=0\\).*  \nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\).  They are all non‑sunny and\nobviously cover every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n*If \\(k\\ge 1\\).*  \n1.  Use the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines  \n\n    \\[\n    x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k .\n    \\tag{2}\n    \\]\n\n    They are non‑sunny and cover all points with first coordinate \\(\\le n-k\\).\n\n2.  The points that are still uncovered are precisely the set  \n\n    \\[\n    T_{k}= \\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le k+1\\}.\n    \\]\n\n    By Lemma 1 there exist \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines that cover \\(T_{k}\\);\n    denote them by \\(L_{1},\\dots ,L_{k}\\).\n\n3.  The family  \n\n    \\[\n    \\mathcal{F}= \\{x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\}\\cup\\{L_{1},\\dots ,L_{k}\\}\n    \\]\n\n    consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, contains precisely the \\(k\\)\n    sunny lines \\(L_{1},\\dots ,L_{k}\\), and covers every lattice point\n    \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\nThus for the chosen \\(k\\) we have produced a configuration satisfying both\nrequirements of the problem.\n\nSince the construction works for every integer \\(k\\) between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\),\nthe set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
