herwise the point lies on a line with a larger\n   index.  A straightforward verification (checking the two possibilities\n   \\(b=1\\) and \\(b\\ge 2\\)) shows that every admissible point satisfies equation\n   (1) for some \\(i\\).  Hence the family \\(\\{L_{1},\\dots ,L_{n}\\}\\) covers all\n   required points, providing a configuration with exactly \\(k=n\\) sunny lines.\n\n3. **Reduction for intermediate \\(k\\).**  \n   Let \\(0<k<n\\).  Consider the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=n-k .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n   They are non‑sunny and cover every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a\\le n-k\\).\n   The points that remain uncovered satisfy \\(a\\ge n-k+1\\) and\n   \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  Writing \\(a'=a-(n-k)\\) (so \\(a'\\ge 1\\)) we obtain\n   \\[\n   a'+b\\le k+1 .\n   \\]\n   Thus the uncovered points are precisely the set\n   \\[\n   \\{(a'+n-k,b)\\mid (a',b)\\in T_{k}\\},\n   \\]\n   i.e. a translate of the triangular set \\(T_{k}\\) defined in Lemma 1.\n\n   Consequently, if Lemma 1 holds for the integer \\(k\\), we can choose the\n   \\(k\\) sunny lines supplied by Lemma 1, translate them by the vector\n   \\((n-k,0)\\), and together with the lines (2) obtain \\(n\\) distinct lines\n   covering every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and containing exactly\n   \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Lemma 1 (open)**  \n*For every integer \\(m\\ge 1\\) there exist \\(m\\) distinct sunny lines that\ntogether contain all lattice points*\n\\[\nT_{m}= \\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le m+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA proof of Lemma 1 would complete the solution and show that the set of\nadmissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct