 coordinate \\(c\\); any vertical line \\(x=c\\) contains all points with\nfirst coordinate \\(c\\); any line of slope \\(-1\\) is of the form \\(x+y=c\\) and\ncontains all points with \\(a+b=c\\).\n\nConsequently, after removing the at most four points covered by \\(L_{1},L_{2}\\),\nthe set \\(\\mathcal T_{n}\\) still contains a whole “border’’ of points:\n\\[\n\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\}\\cup\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\}\\cup\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=n+1\\}.\n\\]\nThese three families are pairwise disjoint, each of them needs a distinct\nnon‑sunny line, and none of them can be covered by a single sunny line.\nThus at least three non‑sunny lines are necessary, which together with the two\nsunny lines gives at least five lines, contradicting the requirement of exactly\n\\(n\\) lines (recall \\(n\\ge 3\\)).  Hence **no configuration with \\(k=2\\) exists**.\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Construction for \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) lines  \n\n\\[\nx+y=2,\\;x+y=3,\\dots ,x+y=n+1 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nEach line in (2) has slope \\(-1\\) and therefore is non‑sunny.\nFor any \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal T_{n}\\) we have \\(a+b\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\), so the\npoint lies on the line \\(x+y=a+b\\).  Thus (2) covers all required points and\ncontains no sunny line.  Hence \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Construction for \\(k=1\\)\n\nUse the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nL_{0}: y=x ,\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nwhich contains the points \\((1,1),(2,2),\\dots ,(\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rfloor,\n\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rfloor)\\).\n\nAdd the \\(n-1\\) non‑sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nx+y=3,\\;x+y=4,\\dots ,x+y=n+1 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal T_{n}\\) with \\(a\\neq b\\) satisfies\n\\(a+b\\ge 3\\) and lies on the line \\(x+y=a+b\\) from (4); the points with\n\\(a=b\\) lie on \\(L_{0}\\).  Thus (3) together with (4) covers all points and\ncontains exactly one sunny line.  Hence \\(k=1\\) is attainable.\n\n---\n\n### 5.  All‑sunny configurations for any size  \n\nWe prove by induction the following statement.\n\n> **Claim.**  \n> For every integer \\(m\\ge 3\\) there exist \\(m\\) distinct sunny lines that\n> together contain every point of the triangle \\(\\mathcal T_{m}\\).\n\n#### Base \\(m=3\\)\n\nThe three sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_{1}&: y=x,\\\\\nL_{2}&: y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52,\\\\\nL_{3}&: y=-2x+3\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nhave slopes \\(1,-\\tfrac12,-2\\) (none equals \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)).\nA direct check shows that the six points of \\(\\mathcal T_{3}\\) are exactly the\nintersection points of the three lines in (5).  Hence the claim holds for\n\\(m=3\\).\n\n#### Inductive step\n\nAssume the claim true for some \\(m\\ge 3\\); let \\(\\mathcal C_{m}\\) be a family of\n\\(m\\) sunny lines covering \\(\\mathcal T_{m}\\).\n\nConsider the larger triangle \\(\\mathcal T_{m+1}\\).  Its “new’’ border consists of\nthe points  \n\n\\[\nB:=\\{(1,m),\\,(2,m-1),\\dots ,(m,1)\\}.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nIn \\(\\mathcal C_{m}\\) exactly two lines pass through the point \\((1,m)\\); denote\nthem by \\(U\\) and \\(V\\).  Replace \\(U\\) and \\(V\\) by the three sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_{a}&: y=x,\\\\\nL_{b}&: y=-\\frac{1}{m}x+m+1,\\\\\nL_{c}&: y=-mx+m+1 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nAll three lines in (7) have slopes different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); they are\npairwise distinct and none coincides with a line of \\(\\mathcal C_{m}\\setminus\\{U,V\\}\\).\n\n*Coverage of the new border.*  \nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le m\\) the point \\((t,m+1-t)\\) lies on the\nline \\(L_{b}\\) if \\(t\\le m\\) and on \\(L_{c}\\) if \\(t\\ge 1\\); the two extreme points\n\\((1,m)\\) and \\((m,1)\\) are also on \\(L_{a}\\).  Hence all points of \\(B\\) are\ncovered by the three new lines.\n\n*Preservation of the old points.*  \nEvery point of \\(\\mathcal T_{m}\\) that was not on \\(U\\) or \\(V\\) remains covered,\nbecause the remaining \\(m-2\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal C_{m}\\) are unchanged.\nThe points of \\(\\mathcal T_{m}\\) that were on \\(U\\) or \\(V\\) are now covered by\n\\(L_{a},L_{b},L_{c}\\) (each of these three lines meets \\(\\mathcal T_{m}\\) in at\nleast one of the former points).  Consequently the new family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal C_{m+1}:=\\bigl(\\mathcal C_{m}\\setminus\\{U,V\\}\\bigr)\\cup\\{L_{a},L_{b},L_{c}\\}\n\\]\n\nconsists of \\((m-2)+3=m+1\\) distinct sunny lines and covers the whole\n\\(\\mathcal T_{m+1}\\).\n\nThus the claim holds for \\(m+1\\), completing the induction.\n\n---\n\n### 6.  Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\)\n\nFix \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n*Non‑sunny part.*  \nTake the \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\ny=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=n-k .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThese lines are non‑sunny and together cover all points of \\(\\mathcal T_{n}\\) whose\nsecond coordinate does not exceed \\(n-k\\).\n\n*Sunny part.*  \nThe points that are still uncovered are precisely  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge 2,\\;b\\ge n-k+1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nIntroduce the change of variables  \n\n\\[\na' = a-1,\\qquad b' = b-(n-k)+1 .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a\\ge 2\\) and \\(b\\ge n-k+1\\), we have \\(a',b'\\ge 1\\); moreover\n\n\\[\na'+b' = (a-1)+(b-(n-k)+1)=a+b-(n-k)\\le (n+1)-(n-k)=k+1 .\n\\]\n\nHence the set \\(S\\) is in bijection with the triangle \\(\\mathcal T_{k}\\) defined\nby (1) with \\(n\\) replaced by \\(k\\).  By Lemma 4 there exist \\(k\\) distinct sunny\nlines that cover \\(\\mathcal T_{k}\\); applying the inverse of the transformation\n(10) yields \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines that cover \\(S\\).\n\n*Putting everything together.*  \nThe family consisting of the \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines (8) and the \\(k\\) sunny\nlines obtained from Lemma 4 contains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, covers every\npoint of \\(\\mathcal T_{n}\\), and has precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nThus for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\) a required configuration exists.\n\n---\n\n### 7.  Conclusion\n\n- Lemma 1 shows that \\(k=2\\) is impossible.\n- Lemmas 2 and 3 give explicit constructions for \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\).\n- Lemma 4 provides, for any size, a configuration with **all** lines sunny;\n  Lemma 5 uses this to obtain configurations with any prescribed number\n  \\(k\\ge 3\\) of sunny lines.\n\nConsequently, for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,3,4,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
