pection shows that all \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\) are attainable; the impossibility of \\(k=2\\) proved in Lemma 1 does **not** apply because the hypothesis \\(n\\ge4\\) fails.  \n  6. **Conclusion.** Combining Lemmas 1–4 yields the exact set of admissible \\(k\\) for each \\(n\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{P}_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nand let a *configuration* be a set \\(\\mathcal{L}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines such that every point of \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal{L}\\).\nA line is called **non‑sunny** if it is parallel to one of the three lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{h}:y=0,\\qquad \\ell_{v}:x=0,\\qquad \\ell_{d}:x+y=0,\n\\]\n\ni.e. its slope belongs to \\(\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\); otherwise it is **sunny**.\nFor a configuration \\(\\mathcal{L}\\) denote by \\(k\\) the number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal{L}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Impossibility of \\(k=2\\) for \\(n\\ge 4\\))\n\nAssume \\(n\\ge 4\\) and that a configuration \\(\\mathcal{L}\\) contains exactly two sunny lines.\nConsequently \\(\\mathcal{L}\\) contains \\(n-2\\) non‑sunny lines.\nLet  \n\n\\[\nR\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\},\\;|R|=r,\\qquad \nC\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\},\\;|C|=c,\\qquad \nD\\subseteq\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\},\\;|D|=d,\n\\]\n\nbe respectively the sets of rows (\\(y\\)-coordinates), columns (\\(x\\)-coordinates) and anti‑diagonals (\\(x+y\\)) that are covered by the non‑sunny lines.\nThus \\(r+c+d=n-2\\).\n\nA point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal{P}_n\\) is *still uncovered* after the non‑sunny lines are removed iff  \n\n\\[\na\\notin C,\\qquad b\\notin R,\\qquad a+b\\notin D. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe number of points satisfying (1) is at least  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nU &\\ge (n-r)(n-c)-d\\,(n-c) \\\\\n  &= (n-c)\\bigl[(n-r)-d\\bigr] \\\\\n  &= (n-c)\\bigl[n-(r+d)\\bigr] \\\\\n  &= (n-c)(c+2) \\qquad\\text{(since }r+d=n-2-c\