ll uncovered* after the non‑sunny lines are removed iff  \n\n\\[\na\\notin C,\\qquad b\\notin R,\\qquad a+b\\notin D. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe number of points satisfying (1) is at least  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nU &\\ge (n-r)(n-c)-d\\,(n-c) \\\\\n  &= (n-c)\\bigl[(n-r)-d\\bigr] \\\\\n  &= (n-c)\\bigl[n-(r+d)\\bigr] \\\\\n  &= (n-c)(c+2) \\qquad\\text{(since }r+d=n-2-c\\text{)} .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(0\\le c\\le n-2\\) the product \\((n-c)(c+2)\\) attains its minimum at the endpoints\n\\(c=0\\) or \\(c=n-2\\); in both cases it equals \\(2n\\).\nHence  \n\n\\[\nU\\ge 2n\\ge 8\\qquad (n\\ge 4).\n\\]\n\nThus at least eight points of \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\) remain uncovered after the \\(n-2\\) non‑sunny lines are taken away.\nAmong these points choose any two distinct rows \\(b_1<b_2\\) and any two distinct columns \\(a_1<a_2\\) that are still present; such rows and columns exist because at least two rows and two columns survive (otherwise \\((n-c)(c+2)\\) would be smaller than \\(2n\\)).\nThe four points  \n\n\\[\n(a_1,b_1),\\;(a_1,b_2),\\;(a_2,b_1),\\;(a_2,b_2)\n\\]\n\nform a \\(2\\times2\\) sub‑grid of uncovered points.\nSelect the three points  \n\n\\[\nP_1=(a_1,b_1),\\qquad P_2=(a_1,b_2),\\qquad P_3=(a_2,b_1).\n\\]\n\nPairs \\((P_1,P_2)\\) and \\((P_1,P_3)\\) share respectively a column and a row, while the pair \\((P_2,P_3)\\) lies on the anti‑diagonal \\(x+y=a_1+b_2=a_2+b_1\\).\nConsequently **any** line that contains two of the three points must be parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\); such a line is non‑sunny.\nSince the configuration contains only two sunny lines, at most two of the three points can be covered by sunny lines, contradicting the requirement that all points be covered.\nTherefore a configuration with exactly two sunny lines cannot exist when \\(n\\ge 4\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Existence for \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\))\n\n*\\(k=0\\).*  \nTake the \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\ny=1,\\;y=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;y=n .\n\\]\n\nEach line is non‑sunny, they are distinct, and every point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal{P}_n\\) has its second coordinate \\(b\\) equal to one of \\(1,\\dots ,n\\); hence it lies on the line \\(y=b\\).\n\n*\\(k=1\\).*  \nUse the \\(n-1\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-1\\) (non‑sunny).  \nThe only point of \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\) not yet covered is \\((1,n)\\).  \nAdd any sunny line passing through \\((1,n)\\); for instance the line  \n\n\\[\ny = -2x + (n+2)\n\\]\n\nhas slope \\(-2\\neq 0,-1,\\infty\\) and is therefore sunny.  \nAll \\(n\\) lines are distinct and the covering condition holds.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Existence for any \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\))\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) satisfying \\(3\\le k\\le n\\).\nLet  \n\n\\[\nh=n-k\\ge 0 .\n\\]\n\n1. **Non‑sunny part.**  \n   Take the \\(h\\) horizontal lines  \n\n   \\[\n   y=1,\\;y=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;y=h .\n   \\]\n\n   They are non‑sunny and cover all points of \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\) whose second coordinate does not exceed \\(h\\).\n\n2. **Sunny part.**  \n   For each remaining row \\(r\\) with \\(h+1\\le r\\le n\\) choose a distinct slope  \n\n   \\[\n   m_r\\in\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0,-1\\}\n   \\]\n\n(e.g. \\(m_r=r\\)).  \n   Define the line  \n\n   \\[\n   L_r:\\; y = m_r\\,(x-1)+r .\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(L_r\\) passes through the lattice point \\((1,r)\\); its slope is neither \\(0\\), \\(-1\\) nor \\(\\infty\\), so \\(L_r\\) is sunny.  \n   Because the slopes \\(m_r\\) are distinct, the \\(k\\) lines \\(\\{L_r\\mid r=h+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) are pairwise distinct.\n\n   For any point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(b=r\\ge h+1\\) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   b = m_r\\,(a-1)+r \\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad a = 1+\\frac{b-r}{m_r}.\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(a\\) is an integer and \\(b=r\\), the equality holds precisely when \\(a=1\\); nevertheless the point \\((1,r)\\) is already on \\(L_r\\