ow every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on at least one of the \\(n\\) lines, and exactly one line is sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Construction with three sunny lines (\\(n\\ge4\\))\n\nLet \\(n\\ge4\\) and put \\(h=n-3\\).\n\n#### 3.1  Non‑sunny lines (total \\(h+2=n-1\\) lines)\n\n1. Horizontal lines \\(y=1, y=2,\\dots , y=h\\).  \n   They cover all points whose second coordinate \\(b\\le h\\).\n\n2. The vertical line \\(x=1\\).  \n\n3. The diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\) (slope \\(-1\\)).  \n\nThese \\(n-1\\) lines are distinct and non‑sunny.\n\n#### 3.2  Points still uncovered\n\nA direct inspection of the rows \\(b=h+1, h+2, h+3\\) (i.e. \\(b=n-2, n-1, n\\)) shows that after the lines above the only points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) not lying on any of them are  \n\n\\[\nP_1=(2,n-2),\\qquad\nP_2=(2,n-3),\\qquad\nP_3=(3,n-3).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nIndeed:\n\n* Row \\(b=n\\) contains only \\((1,n)\\), which lies on the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\).  \n* Row \\(b=n-1\\) contains \\((1,n-1)\\) (on the vertical line) and \\((2,n-1)\\) (on the diagonal).  \n* Row \\(b=n-2\\) contains \\((1,n-2)\\) (vertical) and \\((3,n-2)\\) (diagonal); the point \\((2,n-2)\\) is not on any of the listed non‑sunny lines.  \n* Row \\(b=n-3\\) contains \\((1,n-3)\\) (vertical), \\((4,n-3)\\) (diagonal); the points \\((2,n-3)\\) and \\((3,n-3)\\) are not covered.\n\nThus exactly the three points in (2) remain uncovered.\n\n#### 3.3  Sunny lines covering the three points\n\nChoose three distinct slopes different from \\(0,-1,\\infty\\); for concreteness take \\(2,3,4\\).  \nDefine\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_1 &: y-(n-2)=2\\,(x-2) &&\\text{(passes through }P_1),\\\\\nL_2 &: y-(n-3)=3\\,(x-2) &&\\text{(passes through }P_2),\\\\\nL_3 &: y-(n-3)=4\\,(x-3) &&\\text{(passes through }P_3).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(L_i\\) has slope \\(2,3,4\\) respectively, hence is sunny, the three lines are distinct, and together they cover the three points in (2).\n\n#### 3.4  Verification\n\nAll points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) are now covered: the rows \\(b\\le h\\) by the horizontal lines, rows \\(b=n-2,n-1,n\\) by the vertical and diagonal lines together with the three sunny lines.  \nThe total number of lines is  \n\n\\[\n(h\\text{ horizontals}) + 1\\text{ vertical} + 1\\text{ diagonal} + 3\\text{ sunny}= (n-3)+1+1+3 = n .\n\\]\n\nHence for every \\(n\\ge4\\) a configuration with exactly three sunny lines exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Obtaining any larger number of sunny lines\n\nStarting from the configuration of Lemma 3 (which has exactly three sunny lines), we may replace any of the non‑sunny lines by a sunny line that passes through a point already covered (for example through \\((1,1)\\)).  \nBecause a sunny line can be chosen with an arbitrary slope different from \\(0,-1,\\infty\\), each replacement yields a new configuration with the number of sunny lines increased by one while still covering all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  \nRepeating this operation we obtain configurations with \\(k=4,5,\\dots ,n\\) sunny lines.  \nThus for every \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\) a suitable configuration exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  The case \\(n=3\\)\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_3=\\{(1,1),\\;(1,2),\\;(2,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\n* \\(k=0\\): three horizontal lines \\(y=1,2,3\\).  \n* \\(k=1\\): lines \\(y=1, y=2\\) (non‑sunny) and a sunny line through \\((1,3)\\) (the point \\((1,3)\\) does not belong to \\(\\mathcal P_3\\), so the sunny line may be any line not parallel to the forbidden families; e.g. \\(y=-2x+5\\)). The two horizontal lines already cover all three points, so the sunny line is superfluous but allowed.  \n* \\(k=2\\): use the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) (covers \\((1,1),(2,1)\\)), the vertical line \\(x=1\\) (covers \\((1,1),(1,2)\\)), and a sunny line \\(y=2x-1\\) (covers \\((1,2)\\)). The three lines are distinct and exactly two of them are non‑sunny, i.e. \\(k=2\\).  \n* \\(k=3\\): three distinct sunny lines, for instance  \n  \\[\n  y=2x-1,\\qquad y=-\\tfrac12 x+\\tfrac52,\\qquad y=3x-2,\n  \\]\n  each passing through one of the three points.  \n\nHence all four values \\(k=0,1,2,3\\) are attainable when \\(n=3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Final classification\n\nCombining the lemmas:\n\n* For \\(n=3\\) the admissible values are \\(\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\).\n* For every integer \\(n\\ge4\\) the admissible values are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,3,4,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\nThis completes the determination of all possible numbers \\(k\\) of sunny lines."
