            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **non‑sunny** iff its slope belongs to \\(\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\); otherwise it is **sunny**.  \nWe are asked, for a given \\(n\\), which non‑negative integers \\(k\\) admit a set \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines such that  \n\n* every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\);  \n* exactly \\(k\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Impossibility of two sunny lines when \\(n\\ge 4\\)\n\nAssume that a configuration \\(\\mathcal L\\) with exactly two sunny lines exists for some \\(n\\ge4\\).  \nLet \\(\\mathcal N\\) be the set of non‑sunny lines; then \\(|\\mathcal N|=n-2\\).\n\nEach non‑sunny line belongs to exactly one of the three families  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H=\\{y=\\text{const}\\},\\qquad \n\\mathcal V=\\{x=\\text{const}\\},\\qquad \n\\mathcal D=\\{x+y=\\text{const}\\}.\n\\]\n\nConsequently at most \\(|\\mathcal N|=n-2\\) rows, at most \\(n-2\\) columns and at most \\(n-2\\) anti‑diagonals are covered by \\(\\mathcal N\\).  \nSince there are \\(n\\) rows, \\(n\\) columns and \\(n\\) anti‑diagonals altogether, at least two rows, two columns and two anti‑diagonals remain **uncovered** by \\(\\mathcal N\\).  \n\nChoose two uncovered rows \\(r_1<r_2\\) and two uncovered columns \\(c_1<c_2\\).  \nThe four lattice points  \n\n\\[\nQ_{11}=(c_1,r_1),\\; Q_{12}=(c_1,r_2),\\; Q_{21}=(c_2,r_1),\\; Q_{22}=(c_2,r_2)\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nare not on any non‑sunny line, because each of them lies on an uncovered row, an uncovered column and an uncovered anti‑diagonal.\n\nObserve that any two distinct points among (1) share either a row, a column, or an anti‑diagonal:\n\n* \\(Q_{11}\\) and \\(Q_{12}\\) share column \\(c_1\\);\n* \\(Q_{11}\\) and \\(Q_{21}\\) share row \\(r_1\\);\n* \\(Q_{12}\\) and \\(Q_{22}\\) share row \\(r_2\\);\n* \\(Q_{21}\\) and \\(Q_{22}\\) share column \\(c_2\\);\n* \\(Q_{11}\\) and \\(Q_{22}\\) satisfy \\(c_1+r_1 = c_2+r_2\\) (same anti‑diagonal);\n* \\(Q_{12}\\) and \\(Q_{21}\\) satisfy \\(c_1+r_2 = c_2+r_1\\) (same anti‑diagonal).\n\nHence a line that is **not** parallel to any of the three forbidden families can contain **at most one** of the four points, because containing two would force the line to be parallel to the common row, column, or anti‑diagonal, contradicting its sunniness.\n\nWe have only two sunny lines, so they can cover at most two of the four points in (1).  \nAt least one point of (1) therefore remains uncovered, contradicting the requirement that every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) be covered.  \n\nThus for every \\(n\\ge4\\) a configuration with exactly two sunny lines does **not** exist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Configurations with \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\)\n\n*\\(k=0\\).*  \nTake the \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\ny=1,\\;y=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;y=n .\n\\]\n\nEach line is non‑sunny, they are distinct, and a point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on the line \\(y=b\\). Hence all points are covered.\n\n*\\(k=1\\).*  \nUse the \\(n-1\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-1\\).  \nAll points with second coordinate \\(