#\\{\\text{vertical lines in }\\mathcal L\\},\\;\nd=\\#\\{\\text{diagonal lines }x+y=c\\text{ in }\\mathcal L\\},\n\\]\nand  \n\n\\[\nk=n-(h+v+d)\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nis the number of sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  The value \\(k=2\\) is impossible for \\(n\\ge5\\)\n\nAssume that a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) with exactly two sunny lines exists for some\n\\(n\\ge5\\).  Then \\(h+v+d=n-2\\).\n\nConsider a row \\(b\\) that is **not** among the \\(h\\) horizontal lines.\nIn that row the columns that are not covered by a vertical line are exactly the\n\\(n-v\\) columns whose indices are not among the \\(v\\) vertical lines.\nEach diagonal line meets the row in at most one point, therefore at most \\(d\\)\npoints of the row can be eliminated by the diagonal lines.\nConsequently the row contains at least  \n\n\\[\n(n-v)-d\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) that are not covered by any non‑sunny line.\n\nA sunny line meets a given row in at most one point; with only two sunny\nlines the total number of points that can be covered in that row is at most\n\\(2\\).  Hence (2) forces  \n\n\\[\n(n-v)-d\\le2 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nFrom \\(h+v+d=n-2\\) we have \\(v+d=n-2-h\\).  Substituting this into (3) gives  \n\n\\[\nn-(n-2-h)\\le2\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;h\\le0 .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(h=0\\).  By symmetry the same argument applied to columns yields\n\\(v=0\\); consequently all non‑sunny lines are diagonal and \\(d=n-2\\).\n\nThe \\(d=n-2\\) diagonal lines are of the form \\(x+y=c_i\\) with distinct\nconstants \\(c_i\\).  They cover exactly \\(n-2\\) anti‑diagonals of\n\\(\\mathcal P_n\\); two anti‑diagonals remain uncovered.  The smallest possible\nuncovered anti‑diagonals are \\(c=2\\) (size 1) and \\(c=3\\) (size 2), so at least\nthree points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) stay uncovered.  Two sunny lines can cover at\nmost two points of the two lowest rows \\(b=1,2\\); therefore the three uncovered\npoints cannot be covered by only two sunny lines – a contradiction.\n\nHence for every \\(n\\ge5\\)\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\neq2}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Constructions for the admissible values of \\(k\\)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\ny=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=n .\n\\]\n\nEvery \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on the line \\(y=b\\); all lines are\nnon‑sunny, so \\(k=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  \\(k=1\\)\n\nUse the \\(n-1\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-1\\).  \nAll points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) are covered except \\((1,n)\\).  \nAdd any sunny line through \\((1,n)\\), for instance  \n\n\\[\ny=-2x+n+2 .\n\\]\n\nNow exactly one line is sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3  A correct construction for \\(k=3\\) (all \\(n\\ge5\\))\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nH=\\{y=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=n-3\\}\\qquad (n-3\\text{ horizontal lines}).\n\\]\n\nThe points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) not belonging to a line of \\(H\\) are precisely  \n\n\\[\nR=\\{(a,b)\\mid b\\in\\{n-2,n-1,n\\},\\;a\\le n+1-b\\},\n\\]\n\ni.e. the six points  \n\n\\[\n(1,n),\\;(2,n-2),\\;(1,n-1),\\;(2,n-1),\\;(3,n-2),\\;(1,n-2).\n\\]\n\nConsider the three sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_1 &: y-n = -2(x-1) &&\\text{(through }(1,n)\\text{ and }(2,n-2)),\\\\\nL_2 &: y-(n-1)= -\\tfrac12\\,(x-1) &&\\text{(through }(1,n-1)\\text{ and }(3,n-2)),\\\\\nL_3 &: y-(n-2)= 2\\,(x-2) &&\\text{(through }(2,n-2)\\text{ and }(1,n-2)).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nEach line has slope different from \\(0,-1,\\infty\\); therefore all three are\nsunny and they are pairwise distinct.  A direct check shows that every point of\n\\(R\\) lies on at least one of \\(L_1,L_2,L_3\\).\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{3}=H\\cup\\{L_1,L_2,L_3\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains \\((n-3)+3=n\\) distinct lines,