 \\((1,b),(2,b),\\dots ,(n+1-b,b)\\); there are \\(n+1-b\\) of them.  \n\n   *Vertical lines.*  A vertical line can meet the row in at most one point, so the \\(v\\) vertical lines can cover at most \\(v\\) points of the row.  \n\n   *Diagonal lines.*  A line of the form \\(x+y=c\\) also meets the row in at most one point; the \\(d\\) diagonal lines can therefore cover at most \\(d\\) points of the row.  \n\n   Consequently the non‑sunny lines together cover at most \\(v+d\\) points of the row.  \n   The two sunny lines can each meet the row in at most one point, so they cover at most two further points.  \n   Hence the whole family can cover at most  \n\n   \\[\n   v+d+2\\le (n-2-h)+2=n-h                                   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   points of the row.\n\n   Because the row contains \\(n+1-b\\) points, (2) forces  \n\n   \\[\n   n+1-b\\le n-h\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad b\\ge h+1.            \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Thus **every** row that is not horizontal must have index at least \\(h+1\\).  \n   In particular the rows \\(b=1,2,\\dots ,h\\) must all be horizontal; therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   h\\le n-2-h\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad h\\le\\frac{n-2}{2}.    \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(n\\ge5\\), (4) gives \\(h\\le1\\).  \n\n   *Case \\(h=0\\).*  Then all non‑sunny lines are vertical or diagonal, i.e. \\(v+d=n-2\\).  \n   The \\(v\\) vertical lines can cover at most \\(v\\) distinct columns, and the \\(d\\) diagonal lines can cover at most \\(d\\) distinct anti‑diagonals.  \n   The set \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) contains the anti‑diagonals \\(c=2,3,\\dots ,n\\) (there are \\(n-1\\) of them).  \n   Because \\(v+d=n-2\\), at least one anti‑diagonal is uncovered; the smallest possible uncovered anti‑diagonal is \\(c=2\\) (the point \\((1,1)\\)).  \n   The two sunny lines can each contain at most one point of this anti‑diagonal, so at least one point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) remains uncovered – a contradiction.\n\n   *Case \\(h=1\\).*  The unique horizontal line is \\(y=1\\).  \n   The rows \\(b=2,3,\\dots ,n\\) are not horizontal, so by (3) each of them satisfies \\(b\\ge2\\); this is true.  \n   However the row \\(b=2\\) contains the two points \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,2)\\).  \n   The vertical and diagonal lines together can cover at most \\(v+d=n-3\\) points of this row, while the two sunny lines can cover at most two more, giving at most \\(n-1\\) points.  \n   The row actually contains \\(n-1\\) points, so equality must hold, which forces **every** point of the row to be covered by a distinct non‑sunny line.  \n   In particular the point \\((2,2)\\) must lie on a vertical line, i.e. on the line \\(x=2\\).  \n   But then the point \\((2,1)\\) (which belongs to the horizontal line \\(y=1\\)) is also on \\(x=2\\); consequently the two lines \\(y=1\\) and \\(x=2\\) intersect at \\((2,1)\\), contradicting the requirement that the \\(n\\) lines be distinct.  \n\n   Both cases lead to a contradiction; therefore for every \\(n\\ge5\\)\n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{k\\neq2}.                                            \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Explicit constructions for the admissible values of \\(k\\).**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  Use the \\(n\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n\\).  \n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  Use the \\(n-1\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-1\\) and add the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_{1}:\\;y-n=-2(x-1) \\qquad\\bigl(\\text{slope }-2\\bigr),\n   \\]\n\n   which passes through the only uncovered point \\((1,n)\\).\n\n   *\\(k=3\\) (valid for every \\(n\\ge5\\)).*  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   H=\\{y=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=n-3\\}\\qquad (n-3\\text{ horizontal lines}).\n   \\]\n\n   The six points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) not lying on a line of \\(H\\) are  \n\n   \\[\n   (1,n),\\;(2,n-2),\\;(1,n-1),\\;(2,n-1),\\;(3,n-2),\\;(1,n-2).\n   \\]\n\n   They are covered by the three sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   L_{1}&:\\;y-n=-2(x-1)               &&\\text{slope }-2,\\\\\n   L_{2}&:\\;y-(n-1)=-\\tfrac12(x-1)    &&\\text{slope }-\\tfrac12,\\\\\n   L_{3}&:\\;y-(n-2)=\\;1\\,(x-1)        &&\\text{slope }1 .\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   One checks directly that each of the six points belongs to at least one of\n   \\(L_{1},L_{2},L_{3}\\); the three slopes are different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\),\n   hence the three lines are sunny.  \n   The family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_{3}=H\\cup\\{L_{1},L_{2},L_{3}\\}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of \\((n-3)+3=n\\) distinct lines, exactly three of which are sunny,\n   and it covers the whole set \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n   *From \\(k=3\\) to any larger \\(k\\) (\\(3\\le k\\le n\\)).*  \n   Let \\(t=k-3\\) (\\(0\\le t\\le n-3\\)).  \n   Starting from \\(\\mathcal L_{3}\\) we add the following \\(t\\) sunny lines\n\n   \\[\n   S_{i}:\\;y-(n-2-i)=\\;1\\,(x-1)\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,t),\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. the line of slope \\(1\\) passing through the point \\((1,n-2-i)\\).  \n   Each \\(S_{i}\\) is sunny and distinct from all previously introduced lines.  \n   The point \\((1,n-2-i)\\) (which lies on the horizontal line \\(y=n-2-i\\))\n   is now also covered by \\(S_{i}\\); the second point of that row,\n   \\((2,n-2-i)\\), is already covered by one of the three base sunny lines\n   (\\(L_{1},L_{2}\\) or \\(L_{3}\\)).  Consequently we may delete the horizontal\n   line \\(y=n-2-i\\) without uncovering any point.\n\n   After adding the \\(t\\) lines \\(S_{i}\\) and deleting the \\(t\\) corresponding\n   horizontals we obtain a family of exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-3-t)+3+t=n\n   \\]\n\n   distinct lines, of which  \n\n   \\[\n   3+t=k\n   \\]\n\n   are sunny.  All points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) remain covered, because every\n   point either lies on one of the three base sunny lines, on one of the added\n   sunny lines \\(S_{i}\\), or on one of the remaining horizontal lines.\n\n   Hence for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\) a suitable configuration\n   exists.\n\n4. **The small cases \\(n=3,4\\).**  \n\n   *\\(n=3\\).*  The set \\(\\mathcal P_{3}=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  \n   Direct inspection yields configurations for all four values \\(k=0,1,2,3\\)\n   (e.g. three horizontals for \\(k=0\\); two horizontals plus a sunny line for\n   \\(k=1\\); one horizontal, one vertical, one diagonal for \\(k=2\\); three\n   distinct sunny lines each through a different point for \\(k=3\\)).\n\n   *\\(n=4\\).*  The set \\(\\mathcal P_{4}\\) contains six points.  \n   The constructions of Sections 2.1–2.4 give \\(k=0,1,3,4\\).  \n   For \\(k=2\\) we may use  \n\n   \\[\n   y=1,\\;y=2,\\;x+y=5,\\;\n   \\ell_{1}:\\;y-3=-\\tfrac12(x-1),\\;\n   \\ell_{2}:\\;y-2=2(x-2),\n   \\]\n\n   where \\(\\ell_{1}\\) and \\(\\ell_{2}\\) are sunny and together with the three\n   non‑sunny lines cover all six points.  Thus every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3,4\\}\\) is\n   attainable.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   Combining the impossibility of \\(k=2\\) for \\(n\\ge5\\) with the explicit\n   constructions for all remaining values yields the complete classification\n   stated in the Verdict. ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
