n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nBy the induction hypothesis, \\(T_n=S_{n-1}\\) can be covered by \\(n-1\\) distinct sunny lines. Adding \\(\\ell_n\\) gives a covering of \\(S_n\\) by \\(n\\) sunny lines. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2  \nFor any integers \\(n\\ge 3\\) and \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of them sunny, whose union contains all points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nTake the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThese lines are not sunny. Every point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) with \\(a\\le n-k\\) lies on one of them.  \nThe points that are not yet covered are precisely those with \\(a\\ge n-k+1\\); writing \\(a'=a-(n-k)\\) and \\(b'=b\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\na'+b'\\le k+1,\\qquad a',b'\\ge 1 .\n\\]\n\nThus the uncovered set is exactly \\(S_k\\) (see (2) with \\(n\\) replaced by \\(k\\)).  \nBy Lemma 1, \\(S_k\\) can be covered by \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nTogether with the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines we obtain a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of them sunny, covering all of \\(S_n\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Completion of the proof  \n\nLemma 2 shows that for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) a suitable configuration of \\(n\\) lines exists.  \nConversely, any configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines obviously contains a number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfying \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \n\nHence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,0\\le k\\le n\\, } .\n\\]\n\n∎"
