Apply Lemma\u202f1 with \\(m=k+1\\).  \n   Lemma\u202f1 provides the \\(k\\) sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\; y = -\\,i\\,x + (k+2),\\qquad i=1,2,\\dots ,k .\n   \\tag{12}\n   \\]\n\n   Their union contains all points of \\(S_{k+1}\\) except \\((k+1,1)\\).  \n   After the shift (10) the missing point becomes \\((k+2,1)\\), which is precisely the point excluded from \\(T\\) in (11).  \n   Consequently the lines \\(\\{L_i\\}_{i=1}^{k}\\) cover the whole set \\(T\\).\n\n4. **Adding the last vertical line.**  \n   The point \\((k+2,1)\\) (equivalently \\((n-k,1)\\) in the original coordinates) is not yet covered.  \n   Include the vertical line  \n\n   \\[\n   V_{n-k}:\\; x=n-k .\n   \\tag{13}\n   \\]\n\n   This line is not sunny.\n\n5. **Counting.**  \n   The total family consists of  \n\n   * the \\(n-k-1\\) vertical lines \\(V_1,\\dots ,V_{n-k-1}\\);\n   * the single vertical line \\(V_{n-k}\\);\n   * the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\).\n\n   Hence we have exactly \\( (n-k-1)+1+k = n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely the \\(k\\) lines \\(L_i\\) are sunny.\n\n6. **Verification of the covering condition.**  \n   By construction every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs either to one of the vertical lines (if its first coordinate is \\(\\le n-k\\)) or to one of the sunny lines (if its first coordinate is \\(\\ge n-k\\)).  \n   Therefore the two requirements of the problem are satisfied.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n-1,\n\\]\n\nthere exist \\(n\\) distinct lines in the plane such that  \n\n* each point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one of the lines, and  \n* exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny.\n\nThus the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) certainly contains \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).  \nThe existence (or non\u2011existence) of a configuration with **all** \\(n\\) line