n   lines we may assume that all of them are antidiagonals, because an\n   antidiagonal of sum \\(s\\) contains at least as many points as any vertical\n   or horizontal line (the largest vertical or horizontal line has at most\n   \\(n\\) points, while the antidiagonal with \\(s=n+1\\) has \\(n\\) points).\n\n   Hence the total number of points of \\(T_n\\) that can be covered by the\n   \\(m\\) non‑sunny lines is at most the sum of the \\(m\\) largest numbers among\n   \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).  Denote this maximum by  \n\n   \\[\n   M(m)=\\sum_{j=0}^{m-1}(n-1-j)=\\frac{m\\,(2n-m-1)}{2}.\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   The set \\(T_n\\) contains  \n\n   \\[\n   |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   points.  Consequently the \\(k\\) sunny lines must cover at least  \n\n   \\[\n   |T_n|-M(m)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-\\frac{(n-k)\\,(2n-n+k-1)}{2}\n   =\\frac{k(k+1)}{2}\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   points.\n\n   A sunny line has slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore it can meet\n   each row \\(y=b\\) and each column \\(x=a\\) of the integer lattice in at most one\n   point.  In the triangular region \\(T_n\\) there are exactly \\(n\\) rows (with\n   \\(b=1,2,\\dots ,n\\)) and \\(n\\) columns (with \\(a=1,2,\\dots ,n\\)).  Hence a\n   sunny line can contain at most two points of \\(T_n\\) (one from a row and one\n   from a column), and it can contain **exactly two** points only when those two\n   points are not on the same antidiagonal.  In particular, a sunny line can\n   cover at most two points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n   Therefore the \\(k\\) sunny lines can cover at most \\(2k\\) points.  Combining\n   this with (4) we obtain the necessary inequality  \n\n   \\[\n   2k\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{k(k+1)}{2}\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n   k(k-3)\\le 0 .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Inequality (5) forces \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\).  For \\(n\\ge 4\\) the set\n   \\(T_n\\) contains more than three points, and a direct inspection shows that\n   the cases \\(k=1,2,3\\) cannot be realised (they would require covering at\n   least \\(\\frac{k(k+1)}{2}>k\\) points with only \\(k\\) sunny lines, contradicting\n   the “at most two points per sunny line’’ bound).  Hence for every\n   \\(n\\ge 4\\) the only possible values are \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=n\\).\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly those listed in the boxed statement\n   above.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1,2,3\\}, & n=3,\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,n\\}, & n\\ge 4 .\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
