"### Summary ###\n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI have not found a complete solution to the problem.  \nI have rigorously proved that the integer  \n\n\\[\nk=0\n\\]\n\nis always attainable (by taking \\(n\\) vertical lines, none of which is sunny) and I have clarified the missing verification that every required lattice point is indeed covered by those lines.  \nThe existence (or impossibility) of configurations with any other non\u2011negative integer \\(k\\) remains unresolved; consequently the problem of determining **all** admissible values of \\(k\\) is still open.\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   The set \\(P_n\\) contains \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) points; it is the right\u2011angled triangular lattice that must be covered.\n\n2. **Lemma (coverage by vertical lines).**  \n   For any integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L=\\{L_i\\mid i=1,2,\\dots ,n\\},\\qquad L_i:\\;x=i\n   \\]\n\n   consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, each parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis (hence **not** sunny).  \n   For an arbitrary point \\((a,b)\\in P_n\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); therefore \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation \\(x=a\\) and lies on the line \\(L_a\\).  \n   Consequently every point of \\(P_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n3. **Conclusion of the lemma.**  \n   The construction above shows that the integer  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{k=0}\n   \\]\n\n   belongs to the set of admissible values for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n4. **Remaining values of \\(k\\).**  \n   To obtain a configuration with \\(k>0\\) sunny lines one must replace some of the vertical (or horizontal, or diagonal) lines by lines whose slopes are different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \n   The difficulty is to keep the total number of lines equal to \\(n\\) while still covering every point of \\(P_n\\).  \n   No general construction (or impossibility proof) for arbitrary \\(k\\) has been found; the problem of determining the full set of admissible values of \\(k\\) therefore remains open.  \n\n---  \n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\n**Lemma 1 (existence of a configuration with \\(k=0\\)).**  \nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\\{L_i\\mid i=1,2,\\dots ,n\\},\\qquad L_i:\\;x=i .\n\\]\n\n*Each line \\(L_i\\) is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence it is **not** sunny.*  \n\nTake an arbitrary point \\((a,b)\\in P_n\\). By definition of \\(P_n\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \nSince \\(x=a\\) is precisely the equation of the line \\(L_a\\), the point \\((a,b)\\) satisfies that equation; therefore \\((a,b)\\in L_a\\).  \n\nThus every point of the required set \\(P_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \nThe lines are distinct (their \\(x\\)-intercepts are different) and none of them is sunny.  \nConsequently a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly \\(k=0\\) sunny lines exists for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n**Remark on the unresolved cases.**  \nFor \\(k>0\\) one must supplement (or replace) some of the vertical lines by lines whose slopes are not \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\).  \nThe challenge is to keep the total number of lines equal to \\(n\\) while still covering all points of the triangular lattice \\(P_n\\).  \nAt present no construction that works for every \\(k\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) is known, nor is there a proof that certain values of \\(k\\) are impossible.  \nHence the determination of the complete set of admissible integers \\(k\\) remains an open problem. \u220e"
