cal L_k\\) covers \\(P_n\\)\n\nTake an arbitrary column \\(a\\) (\\(1\\le a\\le n\\)).\n\n*Points supplied by the sunny lines.*  \nFor each \\(i\\in\\{0,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\) the line \\(S_i\\) contains the point  \n\n\\[\n(a,\\;a-i)\n\\]\n\nprovided that the second coordinate is at least \\(1\\). Consequently the points  \n\n\\[\n(a,a),\\;(a,a-1),\\dots ,(a,a-(k-1))\n\\]\n\nthat belong to \\(P_n\\) are all covered by the sunny lines. These are precisely the **top \\(k\\) points** of column \\(a\\).\n\n*Points supplied by the vertical line \\(V_a\\) (when it exists).*  \nIf \\(a\\ge k+1\\) the line \\(V_a\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal L_k\\). Its intersection with \\(P_n\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\{(a,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le a-k\\},\n\\]\n\ni.e. all points of column \\(a\\) whose ordinate does not belong to the top \\(k\\) listed above. Thus the vertical line covers exactly the **remaining points** of that column.\n\n*Columns \\(1,\\dots ,k\\).*  \nWhen \\(a\\le k\\) we have \\(a-k\\le 0\\); therefore column \\(a\\) contains no point with ordinate \\(\\le a-k\\). Hence every point of such a column is already among the top \\(k\\) points and is covered by the sunny lines; no vertical line is needed for these columns.\n\nSince every column of the triangle is handled, every point of \\(P_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\). Hence for each \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Non‑existence for \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\)\n\nWe prove that no configuration with \\(k=n-1\\) or \\(k=n\\) sunny lines can satisfy the covering requirement.\n\n---\n\n**Lemma 1.**  \nA sunny line meets each vertical line \\(x=a\\) in at most one point.\n\n*Proof.*  \nA sunny line is not parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis; therefore its equation can be written as \\(y=m x + c\\) with finite slope \\(m\\neq 0\\). Substituting \\(x=a\\) yields a un