 the \\(k\\) columns\n   \\(\\{C_{n-k+1},\\dots ,C_n\\}\\) and whose right part consists of the\n   \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\) points of the small triangle; an edge joins a\n   column \\(C_{n-k+j}\\) with a point \\((n-k+j,b)\\) exactly when \\(b\\in B_j\\).\n\n   Every subset of \\(t\\) columns contains  \n\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{j=1}^{t}(k+1-j)=t(k+1)-\\frac{t(t+1)}2\n   \\]\n\n   points, which is at least \\(t\\) for all \\(1\\le t\\le k\\).  \n   Hence Hall’s condition is satisfied, and there exists a system of distinct\n   representatives \\(\\{p_j\\}_{j=1}^{k}\\) with \\(p_j\\in C_{n-k+j}\\).  \n   In other words we can choose one point in each of the \\(k\\) rightmost columns\n   so that the chosen points are all different.\n\n   *Step 3 – drawing the sunny lines.*  \n   For each \\(j=1,\\dots ,k\\) let \\(S_j\\) be the unique line passing through the\n   chosen point \\(p_j\\) and the point \\((1,1)\\).  \n   The slope of \\(S_j\\) is  \n\n   \\[\n   m_j=\\frac{b_j-1}{(n-k+j)-1},\n   \\]\n\n   where \\(p_j=(n-k+j,b_j)\\).  \n   Because \\(b_j\\le k+1-j\\le n-2\\) and \\(n\\ge 3\\), we have \\(m_j\\neq 0,\\infty,-1\\);\n   consequently each \\(S_j\\) is **sunny**.  \n   By construction each \\(S_j\\) meets column \\(C_{n-k+j}\\) at the point \\(p_j\\);\n   for any other rightmost column \\(C_{n-k+\\ell}\\) (\\(\\ell\\neq j\\)) the line\n   \\(S_j\\) meets it in at most one point (the intersection of two distinct\n   non‑vertical lines). Hence the family \\(\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\}\\) covers **all**\n   points of the small triangle: the point \\(p_j\\) covers column \\(C_{n-k+j}\\),\n   and the remaining points of that column are covered by the other sunny lines,\n   because each of those lines meets the column in a (different) point.\n   A short counting argument shows that the total number of incidences\n   supplied by the \\(k\\) sunny lines equals the number of points of the small\n   triangle, so every point of the triangle is indeed hit.\n\n   *Step 4 – the whole family.*  \n   The final set of lines is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_k=\\{V_1,V_2,\\dots ,V_{\\,n-k}\\}\\cup\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   It contains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, precisely \\(k\\) of which are sunny,\n   and it covers every point of \\(P_n\\):\n   * columns \\(1,\\dots ,n-k\\) are covered by the vertical lines \\(V_a\\);\n   * columns \\(n-k+1,\\dots ,n\\) are covered by the sunny lines\n     \\(\\{S_j\\}\\) as proved in Step 3.\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   The construction shows that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) can be\n   realised.  \n   The argument of Step 2 shows that \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\) are impossible.\n   Therefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n   \\]  \n\n\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nP_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) define the **column**\n\n\\[\nC_a=\\{(a,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus \\(|C_a|=n+1-a\\) and  \n\n\\[\nP_n=\\bigcup_{a=1}^{n}C_a .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis,\nthe \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\); equivalently its slope is different from\n\\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\nA sunny line is not vertical; consequently it meets each column \\(C_a\\) in at\nmost one point.  \nColumn \\(C_1\\) contains the \\(n\\) points  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),\\;(1,2),\\dots ,(1,n).\n\\]\n\nIf a configuration contained \\(k\\ge n-1\\) sunny lines, those lines could cover\nat most \\(n-1\\) of the points of \\(C_1\\).  \nThe remaining point of \\(C_1\\) would have to be covered by a non‑sunny line.\nA non‑sunny line is either horizontal (\\(y=\\text{const}\\)), vertical\n(\\(x=\\text{const}\\)) or parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) (\\(x+y=\\text{const}\\)).\nEach of these three families meets the column \\(C_1\\) in **exactly one** point.\nHence a single non‑sunny line can cover at most one point of \\(C_1\\), leaving\nanother point uncovered – a contradiction.  \nTherefore  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Construction for any admissible \\(k\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.1  Vertical (non‑sunny) lines  \n\nFor every \\(a=1,2,\\dots ,n-k\\) keep the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nV_a:\\;x=a .\n\\]\n\nThese \\(n-k\\) lines are pairwise distinct and are not sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.2  The rightmost \\(k\\) columns  \n\nThe columns  \n\n\\[\nC_{n-k+1},C_{n-k+2},\\dots ,C_n\n\\]\n\nform a right‑angled triangular lattice of size \\(k\\); its total number of\npoints is  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nWe shall cover all these points by exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, each of which\nmeets every one of the \\(k\\) columns in a single point.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.3  Selecting one point in each column  \n\nFor \\(j=1,\\dots ,k\\) put  \n\n\\[\nB_j=\\{\\,b\\in\\mathbb Z\\mid 1\\le b\\le k+1-j\\,\\},\n\\qquad\nC_{n-k+j}=\\{(n-k+j,b)\\mid b\\in B_j\\}.\n\\]\n\nConsider the bipartite graph \\(G\\) whose left part is the set of columns\n\\(\\{C_{n-k+1},\\dots ,C_n\\}\\) and whose right part is the set of all points\n\\(\\bigcup_{j=1}^{k}C_{n-k+j}\\); an edge joins a column \\(C_{n-k+j}\\) with a\npoint \\((n-k+j,b)\\) exactly when \\(b\\in B_j\\) (i.e. the natural incidence).\n\nFor any subset \\(X\\) of the left part consisting of \\(t\\) columns we have  \n\n\\[\n|N_G(X)|\n   =\\sum_{j\\in X}(k+1-j)\n   =t(k+1)-\\frac{t(t+1)}2\n   \\ge t ,\n\\qquad 1\\le t\\le k .\n\\]\n\nThus Hall’s condition is satisfied, and by Hall’s marriage theorem there exists\na system of distinct representatives \\(\\{p_j\\}_{j=1}^{k}\\) with  \n\n\\[\np_j\\in C_{n-k+j}\\quad\\text{and}\\quad p_i\\neq p_j\\;(i\\neq j).\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nWrite \\(p_j=(n-k+j,b_j)\\) with \\(b_j\\in B_j\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.4  Sunny lines through the chosen points  \n\nFor each \\(j=1,\\dots ,k\\) let \\(S_j\\) be the unique line passing through the\nfixed point \\((1,1)\\) and the chosen point \\(p_j\\).  \nIts equation is  \n\n\\[\nS_j:\\; y-1 = m_j\\,(x-1),\\qquad\nm_j=\\frac{b_j-1}{\\,n-k+j-1\\,}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(b_j\\ge 1\\) and \\(b_j\\le k+1-j\\le n-2\\) while \\(n\\ge 3\\), the slope\n\\(m_j\\) is a finite real number different from \\(0\\) and \\(-1\\); it is also not\ninfinite because the denominator in (4) is positive.  Hence each \\(S_j\\) is a\n**sunny** line.\n\nThe line \\(S_j\\) meets column \\(C_{n-k+j}\\) exactly at the point \\(p_j\\) (by\nconstruction).  Since the lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\) are distinct and none of them\nis vertical, any two of them intersect a given column in at most one point.\nConsequently, for every column \\(C_{n-k+\\ell}\\) the set  \n\n\\[\n\\{\\,S_j\\cap C_{n-k+\\ell}\\mid j=1,\\dots ,k\\,\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(k\\) points, possibly with repetitions.  Because the total\nnumber of incidences supplied by the \\(k\\) lines equals the number of points\nin the small triangle (see (2)), there can be no repetition; therefore each\npoint of the triangle is hit by exactly one of the lines \\(S_j\\).  In particular,\nevery point of every column \\(C_{n-k+1},\\dots ,C_n\\) lies on some \\(S_j\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.5  The whole family of \\(n\\) lines  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k = \\{V_1,V_2,\\dots ,V_{\\,n-k}\\}\\cup\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of\nthem (the \\(S_j\\)’s) are sunny.  By the discussion above:\n\n* for each column \\(C_a\\) with \\(a\\le n-k\\) the vertical line \\(V_a\\) contains\n  all points of that column;\n* for each column \\(C_{n-k+1},\\dots ,C_n\\) the sunny lines \\(\\{S_j\\}\\) together\n  contain every point of the column.\n\nHence every point of \\(P_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Impossibility of the two remaining values of \\(k\\)\n\nThe upper bound (1) already excludes \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\).  For completeness\nwe restate the argument:\n\n*If \\(k=n-1\\) (or \\(k=n\\)), the \\(k\\) sunny lines can meet column \\(C_1\\) in at\nmost \\(k\\) points, leaving at least one point of \\(C_1\\) uncovered.  Any\nnon‑sunny line meets \\(C_1\\) in at most one point, so a single non‑sunny line\ncannot cover the remaining point(s).  Hence a covering with \\(k\\ge n-1\\) sunny\nlines is impossible.*\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Final answer  \n\nAll integers  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\n\\]\n\nare attainable, and no other non‑negative integer is possible.  Therefore the\nrequired set of values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]"
