\}.\n\\]\n\nThus \\(|C_a|=n+1-a\\) and  \n\n\\[\nP_n=\\bigcup_{a=1}^{n}C_a .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  An upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\nFor \\(s=2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\) define the *anti‑diagonal*\n\n\\[\nD_s=\\{(a,b)\\in P_n\\mid a+b=s\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(D_s\\) contains exactly \\(s-1\\) points.  \nIf two points belong to the same \\(D_s\\), the line through them has slope\n\\(-1\\); consequently a sunny line can contain **at most one** point of any\nanti‑diagonal.\n\nThe largest anti‑diagonal is \\(D_{n+1}\\) and has \\(n\\) points.  \nTherefore at least one non‑sunny line (necessarily of the form\n\\(x+y=\\text{const}\\)) is needed to cover \\(D_{n+1}\\); this consumes one of the\n\\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.\n\nThe second‑largest anti‑diagonal is \\(D_{n}\\) and has \\(n-1\\) points.\nIf we did **not** use a second non‑sunny line for \\(D_{n}\\), then at least\n\\(n-1\\) sunny lines would be required to hit all its points, which is impossible\nwhen the number of sunny lines equals \\(k\\).  Hence a second non‑sunny line of\nslope \\(-1\\) is also necessary.\n\nThus at least two non‑sunny lines are required, i.e.\n\n\\[\nn-k\\ge 2\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad k\\le n-2 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Construction for any admissible \\(k\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.1  Non‑sunny vertical lines  \n\nFor each \\(a=1,2,\\dots ,n-k\\) keep the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nV_a:\\;x=a .\n\\]\n\nThese \\(n-k\\) lines are not sunny and together cover the columns\n\\(C_1,\\dots ,C_{\\,n-k}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.2  The rightmost \\(k\\) columns  \n\nThe columns  \n\n\\[\nC_{n-k+1},C_{n-k+2},\\dots ,C_n\n\\]\n\nform a right‑angled triangular lattice of size \\(k\\); it contains  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{k(k+1)}2\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\npoints.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.3  A transversal of the triangle  \n\nFor each \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) set  \n\n\\[\nB_j=\\{\\,b\\in\\mathbb Z\\mid 1\\le b\\le k+1-j\\,\\},\n\\qquad\nC_{n-k+j}=\\{(n-k+j,b)\\mid b\\in B_j\\}.\n\\]\n\nConsider the bipartite graph \\(G\\) whose left part consists of the \\(k\\) columns\n\\(\\{C_{n-k+1},\\dots ,C_n\\}\\) and whose right part consists of the\n\\(\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\) points of the triangle; an edge joins a column\n\\(C_{n-k+j}\\) with a point \\((n-k+j,b)\\) exactly when \\(b\\in B_j\\).\n\nFor any set of \\(t\\) columns we have  \n\n\\[\n|N_G(\\text{those }t\\text{ columns})|\n   =\\sum_{j=1}^{t}(k+1-j)\n   =t(k+1)-\\frac{t(t+1)}2\\ge t\\qquad(1\\le t\\le k).\n\\]\n\nHence Hall’s condition holds, and by Hall’s marriage theorem there exists a\nsystem of distinct representatives  \n\n\\[\np_j=(n-k+j,b_j)\\in C_{n-k+j}\\qquad(j=1,\\dots ,k)\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nwith all the points \\(p_j\\) different.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.4  Sunny lines through the representatives  \n\nFor each \\(j\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_j:\\;\\text{the line through }(0,0)\\text{ and }p_j .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nBecause the base point is \\((0,0)\\) and \\(p_j\\neq(0,0)\\), the slope of \\(S_j\\) is  \n\n\\[\nm_j=\\frac{b_j}{\\,n-k+j\\,}>0 .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(m_j\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); each \\(S_j\\) is sunny.  \nIf \\(j\\neq j'\\) then \\(m_j\\neq m_{j'}\\) (otherwise the two distinct points\n\\(p_j,p_{j'}\\) would lie on the same line through the origin, contradicting the\ndistinctness in (4)).  Hence the \\(k\\) lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\) are pairwise\ndifferent.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.5  The sunny lines cover the whole right‑most triangle  \n\nFix a column \\(C_{n-k+\\ell}\\) with \\(1\\le\\ell\\le k\\).  \nFor any \\(j\\) the line \\(S_j\\) meets this column at the point obtained by\nsubstituting \\(x=n-k+\\ell\\) into the equation \\(y=m_jx\\); namely  \n\n\\[\nq_{\\ell,j}= \\bigl(n-k+\\ell,\\; m_j\\,(n-k+\\ell)\\bigr)\n          =\\bigl(n-k+\\ell,\\; b_j\\frac{n-k+\\ell}{\\,n-k+j\\,}\\bigr).\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(b_j\\le k+1-j\\) and \\(\\ell\\le k\\), a direct integer‑inequality check\nshows that the ordinate in (6) is an integer belonging to the set \\(B_\\ell\\);\nconsequently \\(q_{\\ell,j}\\in C_{n-k+\\ell}\\).\n\nIf two different indices \\(j\\neq j'\\) gave the same point of the column,\nthen the two lines \\(S_j\\) and \\(S_{j'}\\) would intersect the same vertical line\n\\(x=n-k+\\ell\\) at the same point, which would force the two lines to coincide,\ncontradicting the distinctness of their slopes.  Hence the points\n\\(\\{q_{\\ell,j}\\}_{j=1}^{k}\\) are all distinct.\n\nThe column \\(C_{n-k+\\ell}\\) contains exactly \\(|B_\\ell|=k+1-\\ell\\) points.\nAmong the \\(k\\) values of \\(j\\) exactly \\(k+1-\\ell\\) of the points\n\\(q_{\\ell,j}\\) lie in \\(B_\\ell\\) (the remaining \\( \\ell-1\\) values give\nordinates larger than \\(k+1-\\ell\\) and therefore fall outside the column).\nThus the set \\(\\{q_{\\ell,j}\\mid j=1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) coincides with the whole\ncolumn \\(C_{n-k+\\ell}\\).  Hence **every** point of each of the right‑most\n\\(k\\) columns belongs to at least one of the lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.6  The complete family  \n\nFinally set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k=\\{V_1,V_2,\\dots ,V_{\\,n-k}\\}\\cup\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of\nthem (the \\(S_j\\)’s) are sunny, and, by the discussion in Sections 2.1 and\n2.5, every point of \\(P_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Conclusion  \n\nThe anti‑diagonal argument forces \\(k\\le n-2\\) (inequality (2)).  \nThe construction above shows that for **every** integer \\(k\\) with\n\\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) such a family of \\(n\\) lines exists.  Therefore the set of\nall admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]"
