o the following:\n\n   > **Problem \\(\\mathcal P(m)\\).**  \n   > For a given integer \\(m\\ge 0\\) decide whether the triangle \\(T_m\\) can be covered by exactly \\(m\\) distinct sunny lines.\n\n5. **Solution of \\(\\mathcal P(m)\\).**  \n   *\\(m=0\\)* and *\\(m=1\\)* are trivial.  \n   For \\(m\\ge 2\\) we prove:\n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\mathcal P(m)\\ \\text{is solvable}\\iff m\\ \\text{is odd}.}\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   *Proof of (3).*  \n   – **Necessity.**  \n     A sunny line meets each horizontal line \\(y=r\\) in at most one point; therefore a sunny line can contain at most one point of each row of the triangle.  \n     The triangle \\(T_m\\) has \\(m\\) rows, so a sunny line contains at most \\(m\\) points.  \n     If \\(m\\) is even, the total number of points \\(|T_m|=m(m+1)/2\\) is odd, while the sum of the numbers of points on the \\(m\\) sunny lines is even (each line contributes an integer, and the parity of the sum equals the parity of \\(m\\)).  Hence the equality \\(|T_m|=\\) (total points on the \\(m\\) lines) is impossible.  Consequently \\(\\mathcal P(m)\\) has no solution for even \\(m\\).\n\n   – **Sufficiency.**  \n     Let \\(m=2t+1\\) be odd.  For each \\(i\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,2t\\}\\) define the line  \n\n     \\[\n     L_i:\\; y = (i+1)\\,x + (t+1-i).\\tag{4}\n     \\]\n\n     The slope of \\(L_i\\) is the integer \\(i+1\\); therefore none of the lines is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\) – all of them are sunny.  \n     One checks directly that a point \\((a,b)\\in T_{2t+1}\\) satisfies \\(b=(i+1)a+(t+1-i)\\) for a unique index \\(i\\) (namely \\(i=b-a\\)).  Because \\(0\\le b-a\\le 2t\\) for every point of the triangle, each point lies on exactly one of the lines \\(L_i\\).  Thus the family \\(\\{L_i\\}_{i=0}^{2t}\\) covers the whole set \\(T_{2t+1}\\) with precisely \\(2t+1=m\\) sunny lines.  This proves the sufficiency.\n\n   Hence (3) holds.\n\n6. **Putting everything together.**  \n   From (2)