From (2) we see that after using \\(k\\) special lines we are left with the sub\u2011problem \\(\\mathcal P(n-k)\\).  \n   By (3) this sub\u2011problem is solvable exactly when \\(n-k\\) is odd (or \\(n-k=0\\)).  \n   Consequently a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists iff  \n\n   \\[\n   n-k\\in\\{0\\}\\cup\\{\\text{odd integers}\\}.\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   The case \\(k=0\\) (all lines special) is always possible: simply take the \\(n\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n\\).  \n   The case \\(k=n\\) (all lines sunny) is possible precisely when \\(n\\) itself is odd, because then \\(n-k=0\\) and \\(\\mathcal P(0)\\) is trivially true.\n\n   Translating (5) into a description of the admissible \\(k\\) yields the two families stated in the theorem.\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff it is not parallel to any of the three directions  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_h:\\ y={\\rm const},\\qquad \n\\ell_v:\\ x={\\rm const},\\qquad \n\\ell_d:\\ x+y={\\rm const}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Covering by special lines\n\nFor a non\u2011negative integer \\(k\\) let \\(S(k)\\) be the maximal number of points of \\(T_n\\) that can be covered by \\(k\\) special lines.\nChoosing the \\(k\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=k\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nS(k)=\\sum_{r=1}^{k}(n+1-r)=kn-\\frac{k(k-1)}2 .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nNo other choice can give more points, because any two distinct special lines intersect in at most one point of \\(T_n\\); therefore the total number of distinct points covered is at most the sum of the lengths of the chosen lines, and (1) is the maximal possible sum.\n\nConsequently the set of points that remain uncovered after the \\(k\\) special lines is exactly  \n\n\\[\nT_{\\,n-k}=\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\ge1,\\ a+b\\le n-k+1\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich contains  \n\n\\[\n|T_{\\,n-k}|=\\frac{(n-k)(n-k+1)}2 .\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  The auxiliary problem \\(\\mathcal P(m)\\)\n\n> **Problem \\(\\mathcal P(m)\\).**  \n> For a given integer \\(m\\ge0\\) decide whether the triangle \\(T_m\\) can be covered by exactly \\(m\\) distinct sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1 (necessity).\n\nIf \\(m\\ge2\\) is even, \\(\\mathcal P(m)\\) has no solution.\n\n*Proof.*  \nA sunny line meets each horizontal line \\(y=r\\) in at most one point; hence it contains at most one point of each of the \\(m\\) rows of \\(T_m\\).  Thus a sunny line contains at most \\(m\\) points, and the total number of points covered by \\(m\\) sunny lines is at most \\(m^2\\).  \nBut \\(|T_m|=m(m+1)/2\\) is odd when \\(m\\) is even, whereas the sum of the numbers of points on the \\(m\\) lines is even (it is the sum of \\(m\\) integers).  Hence equality cannot hold. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2 (sufficiency).\n\nIf \\(m=2t+1\\) is odd, then \\(\\mathcal P(m)\\) is solvable.\n\n*Proof.*  \nDefine, for each \\(i\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,2t\\}\\),\n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y=(i+1)x+(t+1-i). \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(L_i\\) equals the integer \\(i+1\\); therefore none of the lines is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\); all \\(L_i\\) are sunny.\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_{2t+1}\\).  Because \\(1\\le a\\le 2t+1\\) and \\(1\\le b\\le 2t+2-a\\), the difference \\(b-a\\) satisfies \\(0\\le b-a\\le 2t\\).  Put \\(i=b-a\\).  Substituting \\(i\\) into (3) gives  \n\n\\[\nb=(i+1)a+(t+1-i),\n\\]\n\nwhich is exactly the equation of \\(L_i\\).  Hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_i\\).  The index \\(i=b-a\\) is uniquely determined, so each point of \\(T_{2t+1}\\) belongs to precisely one of the lines \\(L_i\\).  Consequently the family \\(\\{L_i\\}_{i=0}^{2t}\\) covers the whole set \\(T_{2t+1}\\) with exactly \\(2t+1=m\\) sunny lines. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Corollary 3.\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P(m)\\ \\text{has a solution}\\iff m=0\\ \\text{or}\\ m\\ \\text{is odd}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Completion of the original problem\n\nFix \\(n\\ge3\\) and let \\(k\\) be the number of sunny lines in a desired configuration.\nChoose \\(k\\) special lines; by the discussion of \u00a71 the uncovered points are precisely the triangle \\(T_{\\,n-k}\\).  \nThe remaining \\(n-k\\) lines must be sunny and have to cover \\(T_{\\,n-k}\\).  \nBy Corollary\u202f3 this is possible exactly when \\(n-k=0\\) or \\(n-k\\) is odd.\n\nHence a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists **iff**\n\n\\[\nn-k\\in\\{0\\}\\cup\\{\\text{odd integers}\\}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe case \\(k=0\\) is always realizable: take the \\(n\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n\\).  \nIf \\(k=n\\) then \\(n-k=0\\); by (5) this is possible precisely when \\(n\\) itself is odd (the construction of Lemma\u202f2 with \\(m=n\\) gives the required \\(n\\) sunny lines).\n\nWriting (5) explicitly yields the two families announced in the theorem:\n\n*If \\(n\\) is even*,\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0\\}\\cup\\{k\\mid 1\\le k\\le n-1,\\ k\\ \\text{odd}\\}.\n\\]\n\n*If \\(n\\) is odd*,\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1\\}\\cup\\{k\\mid 2\\le k\\le n-1,\\ k\\ \\text{even}\\}\\cup\\{n\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll these values are attainable by the constructions described above (horizontal lines for the special part and the family \\(\\{L_i\\}\\) of Lemma\u202f2 for the sunny part).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Therefore the set of all non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required configuration exists is exactly the set displayed in the boxed answer.**"
