r \\(m\\ge0\\) decide whether the triangle \\(T_m\\) can be covered by exactly \\(m\\) distinct sunny lines.\n\n5. **Solution of \\(\\mathcal P(m)\\).**  \n\n   *Lemma 1 (necessity).*  \n   If \\(m\\ge2\\) is even, \\(\\mathcal P(m)\\) has no solution.  \n   *Proof.*  \n   A sunny line meets each horizontal line \\(y=r\\) in at most one point, so it contains at most one point of each of the \\(m\\) rows of the triangle.  \n   Hence a sunny line contains at most \\(m\\) points, and the total number of points covered by \\(m\\) sunny lines is at most \\(m^{2}\\).  \n   But \\(|T_m|=m(m+1)/2\\) is odd when \\(m\\) is even, whereas the sum of the numbers of points on the \\(m\\) lines is even (it is the sum of \\(m\\) integers).  Therefore equality cannot hold. \u220e\n\n   *Lemma 2 (sufficiency).*  \n   If \\(m=2t+1\\) is odd, then \\(\\mathcal P(m)\\) is solvable.  \n   *Proof.*  \n   We construct \\(m\\) sunny lines inductively.\n\n   *Base case* \\(t=0\\) (\\(m=1\\)).  \n   The single line \\(y=x\\) (slope \\(1\\)) is sunny and passes through the only point \\((1,1)\\) of \\(T_{1}\\).\n\n   *Inductive step.*  \n   Assume that for some odd \\(m=2t+1\\) we have a family \\(\\mathcal L_{m}\\) of \\(m\\) distinct sunny lines covering \\(T_{m}\\).  \n   Consider \\(m+2=2(t+1)+1\\).  \n   The new triangle \\(T_{m+2}\\) consists of \\(T_{m}\\) together with the two outer \u201cborder\u201d rows  \n\n   \\[\n   R_{1}=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le m+2\\},\\qquad\n   R_{2}=\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le m+2\\},\n   \\]\n\n   and the diagonal layer  \n\n   \\[\n   D=\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\ge2,\\ a+b=m+3\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Add the two sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_{h}:\\ y=2x+(m+1),\\qquad\n   L_{v}:\\ y=\\tfrac12x+(m+1).\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Both have slopes \\(2\\) and \\(\\tfrac12\\), which are different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence they are sunny and distinct from every line of \\(\\mathcal L_{m}\\) (all slopes occurring in \\(\\mathcal L_{m}\\) belong to the set \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,m\\}\\cup\\{\\tfrac1{1},\\dots ,\\tfrac1{m}\\}\\) and therefore differ from \\(2\\) and \\(\\tfrac12\\)).  \n\n   One checks directly that  \n\n   * every point of \\(R_{1}\\) satisfies the equation of \\(L_{h}\\);\n   * every point of \\(R_{2}\\) satisfies the equation of \\(L_{v}\\);\n   * every point of the diagonal layer \\(D\\) belongs to exactly one line of \\(\\mathcal L_{m}\\) (because the points of \\(D\\) already belong to \\(T_{m}\\) after translating the coordinates by \\((-1,-1)\\)).\n\n   Consequently the family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_{m+2}= \\mathcal L_{m}\\cup\\{L_{h},L_{v}\\}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of \\((m+2)\\) distinct sunny lines and covers the whole triangle \\(T_{m+2}\\).  \n   This completes the induction, proving that for every odd \\(m\\) a covering by \\(m\\) sunny lines exists. \u220e\n\n   *Corollary 3.*  \n   \\[\n   \\mathcal P(m)\\text{ is solvable }\\iff m=0\\text{ or }m\\text{ is odd}. \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Putting everything together.**  \n\n   After using \\(k\\) special lines we are left with the sub\u2011problem \\(\\mathcal P(n-k)\\).  \n   By (4) this sub\u2011problem is solvable exactly when \\(n-k=0\\) or \\(n-k\\) is odd.  \n   Hence a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists **iff**\n\n   \\[\n   n-k\\in\\{0\\}\\cup\\{\\text{odd integers}\\}. \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   The case \\(k=0\\) (all lines special) is always possible: take the \\(n\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n\\).  \n   The case \\(k=n\\) (all lines sunny) is possible precisely when \\(n\\) itself is odd, because then \\(n-k=0\\) and (4) guarantees a covering of \\(T_{n}\\) by \\(n\\) sunny lines (the construction of Lemma\u202f2 with \\(m=n\\) gives it).\n\n   Translating condition (5) into an explicit description of the admissible \\(k\\) yields the two families stated in the theorem:\n\n   *If \\(n\\) is even*,\n   \\[\n   k\\in\\{0\\}\\cup\\{k\\mid 1\\le k\\le n-1,\\;k\\text{ odd}\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   *If \\(n\\) is odd*,\n   \\[\n   k\\in\\{0,1\\}\\cup\\{k\\mid 2\\le k\\le n-1,\\;k\\text{ even}\\}\\cup\\{n\\}.\n   \\]\n\nAll these values are attainable by the explicit constructions described above (horizontal/vertical/diagonal lines for the special part and the inductively built sunny families for the sunny part).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff it is not parallel to any of the three directions  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_h:\\ y={\\rm const},\\qquad \n\\ell_v:\\ x={\\rm const},\\qquad \n\\ell_d:\\ x+y={\\rm const}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Covering by special lines\n\nFor a non\u2011negative integer \\(k\\) let \\(S(k)\\) be the maximal number of points of \\(T_n\\) that can be covered by \\(k\\) special lines.\nChoosing the \\(k\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=k\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nS(k)=\\sum_{r=1}^{k}(n+1-r)=kn-\\frac{k(k-1)}2 .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nNo other choice can give more points, because any two distinct special lines intersect in at most one point of \\(T_n\\); therefore the total number of distinct points covered is at most the sum of the lengths of the chosen lines, and (1) is the maximal possible sum.\n\nConsequently the set of points that remain uncovered after the \\(k\\) special lines is exactly  \n\n\\[\nT_{\\,n-k}=\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\ge1,\\ a+b\\le n-k+1\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich contains  \n\n\\[\n|T_{\\,n-k}|=\\frac{(n-k)(n-k+1)}2 .\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  The auxiliary problem \\(\\mathcal P(m)\\)\n\n> **Problem \\(\\mathcal P(m)\\).**  \n> For a given integer \\(m\\ge0\\) decide whether the triangle \\(T_m\\) can be covered by exactly \\(m\\) distinct sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1 (necessity).\n\nIf \\(m\\ge2\\) is even, \\(\\mathcal P(m)\\) has no solution.\n\n*Proof.*  \nA sunny line meets each horizontal line \\(y=r\\) in at most one point; hence it contains at most one point of each of the \\(m\\) rows of the triangle.  \nThus a sunny line contains at most \\(m\\) points, and the total number of points covered by \\(m\\) sunny lines is at most \\(m^{2}\\).  \nBut \\(|T_m|=m(m+1)/2\\) is odd when \\(m\\) is even, whereas the sum of the numbers of points on the \\(m\\) lines is even (it is the sum of \\(m\\) integers).  Hence equality cannot hold. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2 (sufficiency).\n\nIf \\(m=2t+1\\) is odd, then \\(\\mathcal P(m)\\) is solvable.\n\n*Proof.*  \nWe construct a covering by induction on \\(t\\).\n\n*Base case* \\(t=0\\) (\\(m=1\\)).  \nThe single line \\(y=x\\) (slope \\(1\\)) is sunny and passes through the only point \\((1,1)\\) of \\(T_{1}\\).\n\n*Inductive step.*  \nAssume that for some odd \\(m=2t+1\\) we have a family \\(\\mathcal L_{m}\\) of \\(m\\) distinct sunny lines covering \\(T_{m}\\).  \nConsider \\(m+2=2(t+1)+1\\).  \nThe new triangle \\(T_{m+2}\\) consists of \\(T_{m}\\) together with the two outer border rows  \n\n\\[\nR_{h}=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le m+2\\},\\qquad\nR_{v}=\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le m+2\\},\n\\]\n\nand the diagonal layer  \n\n\\[\nD=\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\ge2,\\ a+b=m+3\\}.\n\\]\n\nAdd the two sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nL_{h}:\\ y=2x+(m+1),\\qquad\nL_{v}:\\ y=\\tfrac12x+(m+1). \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nBoth have slopes \\(2\\) and \\(\\tfrac12\\), which are different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence they are sunny.  \nTheir slopes are also different from the slopes of the lines in \\(\\mathcal L_{m}\\) (the latter belong to the set \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,m\\}\\cup\\{\\tfrac1{1},\\dots ,\\tfrac1{m}\\}\\)), so the four families are pairwise distinct.\n\nA direct verification shows that  \n\n* every point of \\(R_{h}\\) satisfies the equation of \\(L_{h}\\);\n* every point of \\(R_{v}\\) satisfies the equation of \\(L_{v}\\);\n* every point of the diagonal layer \\(D\\) already belongs to \\(T_{m}\\) after translating the coordinates by \\((-1,-1)\\), and therefore is covered by a line of \\(\\mathcal L_{m}\\).\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{m+2}= \\mathcal L_{m}\\cup\\{L_{h},L_{v}\\}\n\\]\n\nis a family of \\((m+2)\\) distinct sunny lines covering the whole triangle \\(T_{m+2}\\).  \nThis completes the induction, proving that for every odd \\(m\\) a covering by \\(m\\) sunny lines exists. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Corollary 3.\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P(m)\\text{ is solvable }\\iff m=0\\text{ or }m\\text{ is odd}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Completion of the original problem\n\nFix \\(n\\ge3\\) and let \\(k\\) be the number of sunny lines in a desired configuration.\nChoose \\(k\\) special lines; by the discussion of \u00a71 the uncovered points are precisely the triangle \\(T_{\\,n-k}\\).  \nThe remaining \\(n-k\\) lines must be sunny and have to cover \\(T_{\\,n-k}\\).  \nBy Corollary\u202f3 this is possible exactly when \\(n-k=0\\) or \\(n-k\\) is odd.\n\nHence a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists **iff**\n\n\\[\nn-k\\in\\{0\\}\\cup\\{\\text{odd integers}\\}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe case \\(k=0\\) (all lines special) is always realizable: take the \\(n\\) horizontal lines\n\\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n\\); each point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) lies on the line \\(y=b\\).\n\nIf \\(k=n\\) (all lines sunny) then \\(n-k=0\\); by (4) this is possible precisely when \\(n\\) itself is odd.  \nFor odd \\(n\\) the construction of Lemma\u202f2 with \\(m=n\\) provides the required \\(n\\) sunny lines.\n\nWriting condition (5) explicitly gives the two families announced in the theorem:\n\n*If \\(n\\) is even*,\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0\\}\\cup\\{k\\mid 1\\le k\\le n-1,\\;k\\text{ odd}\\}.\n\\]\n\n*If \\(n\\) is odd