+y=n+1\\), which indeed covers all of \\(H\\).\n\nNow consider the interior set  \n\n\\[\nI_n=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nEvery point of \\(I_n\\) lies in a column \\(x=a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n-1\\) and in a row \\(y=b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n-1\\).  \nA sunny line can intersect a given column (or row) at **most one** point, because otherwise two points of the same column would determine a vertical line, which is non‑sunny.  \nConsequently a single sunny line can cover at most one point of each column and at most one point of each row.\n\nSince there are \\(n-1\\) columns (and the same number of rows) that contain points of \\(I_n\\), covering all points of \\(I_n\\) would require at least \\(n-1\\) distinct lines if only sunny lines were used.  \nBut we have only \\(n-1\\) sunny lines in total, and one of them is already the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\), which does **not** intersect any point of \\(I_n\\).  \nThus the remaining \\(n-2\\) sunny lines would have to cover all of \\(I_n\\), which is impossible because \\(I_n\\) contains more than \\(n-2\\) points (indeed \\(|I_n|= \\frac{(n-1)n}{2}>n-2\\) for \\(n\\ge 3\\)).  \n\nHence our assumption is false; at least two lines of \\(\\mathcal F\\) must be non‑sunny. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Corollary 3 (Upper bound for \\(k\\))\n\nIf a configuration satisfies the conditions of the problem, the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines obeys  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe configuration contains exactly \\(n\\) lines, of which at least two are non‑sunny by Lemma 2.  \nTherefore the number of sunny lines is at most \\(n-2\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies \\(1\\le a\\le n\\), hence lies on \\(L_a\\).  \nAll lines are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, so none is sunny.  \nThus \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for \\(k=1\\)\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(D:\\;x+y=n+1\\) (non‑sunny diagonal);\n* \\(V:\\;x=1\\) (non‑sunny vertical);\n* \\(H:\\;y=1\\) (non‑sunny horizontal);\n* For each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n\\) define a sunny line \\(S_c\\) passing through the point \\((c,2)\\) with slope \\(\\displaystyle m_c=\\frac{c-1}{c-2}\\) (any slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) and distinct for different \\(c\\) works).\n\nThe family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F=\\{D,V,H\\}\\cup\\{S_c\\mid 2\\le c\\le n\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines.  \nAll points with \\(b=1\\) lie on \\(H\\); all points with \\(a=1\\) lie on \\(V\\); all points with \\(a+b=n+1\\) lie on \\(D\\).  \nA remaining point \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(a\\ge 2,\\;b\\ge 2\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \nIf \\(b=2\\) then \\((a,2)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(S_a\\) by construction.  \nIf \\(b\\ge 3\\) then \\(a\\le n-1\\) and consequently \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(a+b=n+1\\), so the point is already on \\(D\\).  \n\nHence every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal F\\).  \nExactly one line of \\(\\mathcal F\\) (any chosen \\(S_c\\)) is sunny, giving \\(k=1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Partial Conclusion\n\n* The admissible values of \\(k\\) satisfy  \n  \\[\n  0\\le k\\le n-2 .\n  \\]\n* The extreme values \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are realizable for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n* The values \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\) are impossible (by Corollary 3).\n\nThe existence (or non‑existence) of configurations with \\(k=2,3,\\dots ,n-2\\) remains open.  Further work would be required to either construct such families for each intermediate \\(k\\) or to prove additional restrictions.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step ver