            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_{3}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le4\\}\n      =\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Sunny lines intersect the hypotenuse at most once)\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nH=\\{(a,b)\\in T_{3}\\mid a+b=4\\}= \\{(1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nIf a line \\(L\\) is sunny then \\(|L\\cap H|\\le1\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume \\(L\\) contains two distinct points \\((a_{1},b_{1}), (a_{2},b_{2})\\in H\\).  \nBoth satisfy \\(a_{i}+b_{i}=4\\); hence  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{b_{2}-b_{1}}{a_{2}-a_{1}}=-1,\n\\]\n\nso the slope of \\(L\\) is \\(-1\\), contradicting the definition of a sunny line. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nBecause \\(|H|=3\\) and each sunny line can meet \\(H\\) in at most one point, at least two of the three available lines must be non‑sunny in order to cover all points of \\(H\\).  \nConsequently the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\nk\\le 3-2 = 1. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThus only the values \\(k=0\\) or \\(k=1\\) can possibly occur.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the three vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_{1}:x=1,\\qquad L_{2}:x=2,\\qquad L_{3}:x=3 .\n\\]\n\nEvery point of \\(T_{3}\\) has abscissa \\(1,2\\) or \\(3\\), so it lies on one of these lines.  \nAll three lines are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence none is sunny.  \nTherefore \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for \\(k=1\\)\n\nConsider the following three distinct lines:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_{1}&: x+y=4 \\qquad\\text{(non‑sunny, contains all points of }H),\\\\\nL_{2}&: x=1      \\qquad\\text{(non‑sunny, contains }(1,1),(1,2),(1,3)),\\\\\nL_{3}&: y=\\tfrac12 x \\qquad\\text{(sunny, contains }(2,1)\\text{ only)}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nVerification of coverage:\n\n* Points \\((1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\) belong to \\(L_{1}\\).\n* Points \\((1,1),(1,2)\\) belong to \\(L_{2}\\).\n* Point \\((2,1)\\) belongs to \\(L_{3}\\).\n\nThus every element of \\(T_{3}\\) lies on at least one of the three lines, and exactly one line (\\(L_{3}\\)) is sunny.  \nHence \\(k=1\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Impossibility of \\(k=2\\)\n\nAssume a configuration with exactly two sunny lines and one non‑sunny line.  \nBy Lemma 1 the three points of \\(H\\) cannot be covered by the two sunny lines; therefore the non‑sunny line must be the diagonal \\(x+y=4\\), which covers all of \\(H\\).\n\nThe remaining points to be covered are \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\).  \nA sunny line can contain at most one point of a given column (or row), because two points with the same \\(x\\) (or the same \\(y\\)) would force a vertical (horizontal) line, which is non‑sunny.  \nHence the two sunny lines can cover at most two of the three points lying in column \\(x=1\\).  \nOne point of \\(\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\) would remain uncovered – a contradiction.  \nTherefore no configuration with \\(k=2\\) exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Impossibility of \\(k=3\\)\n\nIf all three lines are sunny, Lemma 1 forces each of the three points of \\(H\\) to lie on a different line; consequently the three lines are already “used’’ to cover exactly those three diagonal points.  \nNo line remains to cover the other three points \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\).  \nThus a configuration with three sunny lines cannot exist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nFor \\(n=3\\) the only feasible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0\\quad\\text{and}\\quad k=1}.\n\\]\n\nAll other values \\(k=2,3\\) are impossible, completing the exhaustive analysis for the minimal case.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
