            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  Preliminaries  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(T_n\\) consists of all lattice points inside (and on) the right‑angled triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\).  \nIts cardinality is  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nThe **hypotenuse** of the triangle is  \n\n\\[\nH_n=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b=n+1\\},\n\\qquad |H_n|=n .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).  \n\n---\n\n### 2.  Lemma 1 – Interaction of sunny lines with the hypotenuse  \n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma 1.}\\qquad\\text{If }L\\text{ is sunny, then }|L\\cap H_n|\\le1 .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume that \\(L\\) contains two distinct points \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2)\\in H_n\\).  \nBoth satisfy \\(a_i+b_i=n+1\\); hence  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1}= \\frac{-(a_2-a_1)}{a_2-a_1}=-1 .\n\\]\n\nThus the slope of \\(L\\) equals \\(-1\\), contradicting the definition of a sunny line. ∎\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Upper bound on the number of sunny lines  \n\nLet a family \\(\\mathcal L=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines cover all points of \\(T_n\\).  \nDenote by \\(k\\) the number of sunny lines among them.\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that \\(k\\ge2\\).\n\n#### 3.1  The diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\) is used  \n\nIf the non‑sunny line \\(D:x+y=n+1\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal L\\), then all points of \\(H_n\\) are already covered by \\(D\\).  \nThe remaining \\(n-1\\) lines must cover the set  \n\n\\[\nT_n\\setminus H_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n\\}=T_{\\,n-1}.\n\\]\n\nThus we obtain a covering of the smaller triangle \\(T_{\\,n-1}\\) by exactly \\(n-1\\) lines that contains at least \\(k\\ge2\\) sunny lines.  \nRepeating the same argument for \\(n-1\\) yields an infinite descent, which is impossible because the process would eventually require a covering of \\(T_2\\) (a set of three points) by fewer than three lines with two sunny lines – a direct check shows this cannot happen.  \nHence the diagonal cannot be present when \\(k\\ge2\\).\n\n#### 3.2  The diagonal is **not** used  \n\nConsequently every point of \\(H_n\\) must be covered by a vertical line \\(x=i\\) or a horizontal line \\(y=j\\).  \nA vertical line meets \\(H_n\\) in exactly one point, namely \\((i,n+1-i)\\); the same holds for a horizontal line.  \nSince a sunny line meets \\(H_n\\) in at most one point (Lemma 1), at most \\(k\\) points of \\(H_n\\) are covered by sunny lines.  \nTherefore at least \\(n-k\\) points of \\(H_n\\) need distinct vertical or horizontal lines.  \nThus we need at least \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines **in addition** to the \\(k\\) sunny ones, i.e. at least \\(n\\) lines altogether.\n\nBecause we have exactly \\(n\\) lines, all of them are already accounted for by covering the points of \\(H_n\\).  \nNo line remains to cover any interior point of \\(T_n\\) (for instance \\((1,1)\\)), a contradiction.\n\nBoth cases lead to a contradiction, so the assumption \\(k\\ge2\\) is false. Hence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\le1}.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Constructions attaining the bounds  \n\n#### 4.1  \\(k=0\\) (no sunny line)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) has abscissa \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\), therefore lies on \\(L_a\\).  \nAll these lines are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, so none is sunny.  \nThus \\(k=0\\) is realizable for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n#### 4.2  \\(k=1\\) (exactly one sunny line)\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nm=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nConsider the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nL_{\\text{sun}}:\\;y=x\\qquad(\\text{slope }1).\n\\]\n\nIt contains the points \\((i,i)\\) for \\(1\\le i\\le m\\) (all of them belong to \\(T_n\\) because \\(2i\\le n+1\\)).  \n\nNow cover the remaining points of \\(T_n\\) by  \n\n* vertical lines \\(x=j\\) for \\(j=m+1,m+2,\\dots ,n\\);  \n* horizontal lines \\(y=t\\) for \\(t=1,2,\\dots ,m-1\\).\n\n**Verification of coverage.**  \n\n*Points on the hypotenuse.*  \nFor each column \\(j\\ge m+1\\) the point \\((j,n+1-j)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=j\\).  \nFor each column \\(i\\le m\\) the hypotenuse point \\((i,n+1-i)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(y=n+1-i\\); this horizontal line is among the set \\(y=t\\) because \\(n+1-i\\le n-m\\le m-1\\) (the inequality follows from the definition of \\(m\\)).  \n\n*Points below the hypotenuse.*  \nIf a point \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(b<a\\le m\\), then \\(b\\le m-1\\) and it lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\).  \nIf \\(a>m\\) (hence \\(b\\le n+1-a\\le n-m\\le m-1\\)), the point lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\).  \n\n*Points on the line \\(y=x\\).*  \nAll points \\((i,i)\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le m\\) are covered by the sunny line \\(L_{\\text{sun}}\\).\n\nThus every element of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one of the \\(n\\) constructed lines, and exactly one of them (\\(L_{\\text{sun}}\\)) is sunny.  \n\nHence \\(k=1\\) is also attainable for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Conclusion  \n\nWe have proved that for any integer \\(n\\ge3\\)\n\n* no configuration can contain two or more sunny lines (\\