            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  Preliminaries  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nGeometrically, \\(T_n\\) is the set of lattice points inside (and on) the right‑angled triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\).  \nIts “hypotenuse’’ is  \n\n\\[\nH_n=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b=n+1\\},\n\\qquad |H_n|=n .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n---\n\n### 2.  Lemma 1 – Interaction of sunny lines with the hypotenuse  \n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma 1.}\\qquad\\text{If }L\\text{ is sunny, then }|L\\cap H_n|\\le1 .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume that \\(L\\) contains two distinct points \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2)\\in H_n\\).  \nBoth satisfy \\(a_i+b_i=n+1\\); hence  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1}\n   =\\frac{-(a_2-a_1)}{a_2-a_1}=-1 .\n\\]\n\nThus the slope of \\(L\\) equals \\(-1\\), contradicting the definition of a sunny line. ∎\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Upper bound on the number of sunny lines  \n\nSuppose a family \\(\\mathcal L=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers all points of \\(T_n\\), and let \\(k\\) be the number of sunny lines among them.\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that \\(k\\ge2\\).\n\n#### 3.1  The diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\) is used  \n\nIf the line  \n\n\\[\nD:\\;x+y=n+1\n\\]\n\nbelongs to \\(\\mathcal L\\), then every point of \\(H_n\\) is already covered by \\(D\\).  \nThe remaining \\(n-1\\) lines must cover the interior set  \n\n\\[\nT_n\\setminus H_n = T_{\\,n-1}\n   =\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus we would have a covering of \\(T_{\\,n-1}\\) by \\(n-1\\) lines that still contains at least two sunny lines.  \nRepeating the same argument for the smaller triangle \\(T_{\\,n-1}\\) would eventually require a covering of \\(T_2\\) (three points) by fewer than three lines with two sunny lines, which is impossible (a direct inspection shows that any covering of those three points needs at least three lines, and at most one of them can be sunny by Lemma 1).  \nHence the diagonal cannot be present when \\(k\\ge2\\).\n\n#### 3.2  The diagonal is **not** used  \n\nConsequently every point of \\(H_n\\) must be covered by a vertical line \\(x=i\\) or a horizontal line \\(y=j\\).  \nA vertical line meets \\(H_n\\) in exactly one point \\((i,n+1-i)\\); a horizontal line meets it in exactly one point \\((n+1-j,j)\\).  \nBy Lemma 1 a sunny line meets \\(H_n\\) in at most one point, so at most \\(k\\) points of \\(H_n\\) are covered by sunny lines.  \nTherefore at least \\(n-k\\) points of \\(H_n\\) need distinct vertical or horizontal lines.  \nThus we need at least \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines **in addition** to the \\(k\\) sunny ones, i.e. at least \\(n\\) lines altogether.\n\nBecause we have exactly \\(n\\) lines, all of them are already accounted for by covering the hypotenuse; no line remains to cover any interior point of \\(T_n\\) (for instance \\((1,1)\\)). This contradiction shows that the assumption \\(k\\ge2\\) is false.\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\le1}.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Constructions attaining the bound  \n\n#### 4.1  The case \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) has abscissa \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\), so it lies on \\(L_a\\).  \nAll these lines are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, therefore none is sunny.  \nThus \\(k=0\\) is realizable for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n#### 4.2  The case \\(k=1\\)\n\nDefine the following family of \\(n\\) lines:\n\n* the diagonal (non‑sunny)  \n  \\[\n  D:\\;x+y=n+1;\n  \\]\n\n* for each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n-2\\) the vertical line  \n  \\[\n  V_i:\\;x=i;\n  \\]\n\n* one sunny line  \n  \\[\n  S:\\;y = x-(n-2) \\qquad\\text{(slope }1\\text{)} .\n  \\]\n\nThe family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\\{D\\}\\cup\\{V_i\\mid 1\\le i\\le n-2\\}\\cup\\{S\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly  \n\n\\[\n1\\;(\\text{diagonal})\\;+\\;(n-2)\\;(\\text{vertical})\\;+\\;1\\;(\\text{sunny}) = n\n\\]\n\ndistinct lines, and only \\(S\\) is sunny.\n\n**Verification that every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on a line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).**  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n*If \\(a\\le n-2\\):* the point belongs to the vertical line \\(V_a\\).\n\n*If \\(a=n-1\\):* the inequality \\(a+b\\le n\\) forces \\(b=1\\).  \n  Substituting \\(a=n-1\\) into the equation of \\(S\\) gives \\(y=(n-1)-(n-2)=1\\); hence \\((n-1,1)\\) lies on \\(S\\).\n\n*If \\(a=n\\):* then \\(b\\ge1\\) would give \\(a+b\\ge n+1\\), contradicting the definition of \\(T_n\\); thus this case does not occur.\n\nConsequently every lattice point required by the problem is covered.  \nExactly one line (\\(S\\)) is sunny, so \\(k=1\\) is attainable for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Conclusion  \n\nWe have proved that no configuration can contain two or more sunny lines (\\(k\\le1\\)).  \nExplicit constructions show that both \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are achievable for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\).  \n\nTherefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,\\,1\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary